User opens the static website
The frontend is served from Static Website Hosting.
User submits email and password
Your frontend sends the credentials to your backend app.
The docs do not define a backend framework, so this part is yours to implement.
Backend checks the database
Store users in PostgreSQL.
Create users or roles with LOGIN privileges using SQL.
See Manage Users and Databases.
Backend verifies the password
If the credentials match, create a session or token in your app.
The docs show PostgreSQL password handling, including password changes through the user endpoint in Change the Login Password.
User reaches protected pages
Use the session or token to gate rental dashboards, bookings, or profile pages.
Protected data stays in PostgreSQL
Set up the cluster in a private LAN.
The database is accessed with the username and password set during creation.
See Set Up a PostgreSQL Cluster.
